      "decimal-coefficients"
    ],
    "taskType": "three-act",
    "btcFit": "Strong three-act structure. Act 1 estimation is the hook — gets every kid into the problem with no math anxiety. Act 2 is the modeling diamond — the 'used' is 41 GB and the rate is 0.18, but the limit is 64 GB total, which catches groups who try 0.18m <= 64. Act 3 questions add concrete time pressure and 'what if' variation.",
    "anticipatedApproaches": "- Q1: Estimates will vary widely — 30 min, 1 hour, 2 hours. That's the point.\n- Q2: 41 + 0.18m <= 64.\n- Q3: 0.18m <= 23, so m <= 127.78 minutes. Round down: 127 full minutes (you can't record a partial minute and have it 'fit' cleanly, but some groups will accept 127.78).\n- Q4: 127 minutes = 2 hours, 7 minutes. (127.78 ≈ 2h 7m 47s.)\n- Q5: 1h 45m = 105 minutes. 127 > 105, so YES the phone makes it. Extra time: 127 - 105 = 22 minutes of buffer.\n- Q6: 41 - 8 + 0.18m <= 64, so 0.18m <= 31, m <= 172.22. Round to 172 minutes (2h 52m). Extra time gained: 172 - 127 = 45 more minutes (and 8 / 0.18 ≈ 44.4 — close!).\n- Q7: SD uses 1/3 the storage per minute, so prediction: about 3x longer. Solving: 0.06m <= 23, m <= 383.33 minutes (about 6h 23m). 383 / 127 ≈ 3.0 — prediction confirmed.",
    "commonMisconceptions": "- Writing 0.18m <= 64 (forgetting the 41 GB already used).\n- Writing 0.18m <= 41 (using the WRONG number — the used storage, not the available).\n- Solving 23 / 0.18 incorrectly: getting 12.78 (decimal placement) or 414 (forgot to divide).\n- For Q5, comparing 127 minutes to 1 hour 45 minutes without converting — saying '127 is bigger than 145' incorrectly, or saying 'no it's only 127' without realizing 105 < 127.\n- For Q7, predicting '0.06 is half of 0.18' when it's actually 1/3 — leading to a 2x prediction instead of 3x.",
    "discussionQuestions": "- How close was your Act 1 estimate to the actual answer? Whose estimate was closest and what reasoning helped?\n- 41 + 0.18m <= 64 vs. 0.18m <= 23. Are these the same inequality? Why?\n- In Q6, clearing 8 GB bought him 45 minutes. Is that 'a lot' or 'not much' given the dance is 1h 45m? How does that compare to switching to SD?\n- Why does the 3x prediction in Q7 work BEFORE solving? What is the relationship between the rate and the time?",
    "extensions": "- Isaiah's friend has the same phone but starts the night with 28 GB used. Write and solve an inequality for her HD recording time. Plot both Isaiah's and her remaining time on a number line. Who gets shut out first?\n- Suppose every 10 minutes the phone auto-deletes the lowest-quality 0.05 GB temp file. Write a piecewise inequality or step model. Does this realistically extend recording time?\n- Storage costs about $0.10 per GB on the cloud. If Isaiah uploads 18 GB after the dance and pays monthly, write an inequality for how many months he can afford it on a $5 allowance.\n- Real check: HD video on iPhone is closer to 0.06 GB/min and 4K is closer to 0.35 GB/min. Solve the problem for both. Which assumption matched the original?",
    "sampleSolutions": "1. (Estimates vary — value the reasoning.)\n2. 41 + 0.18m <= 64.\n3. 0.18m <= 23, so m <= 127.78 minutes.\n4. 127.78 minutes ≈ 2 hours, 7 minutes, 47 seconds.\n5. 1h 45m = 105 min. 127.78 > 105, so YES, the phone outlasts the dance with about 22 min 47 s of buffer.\n6. With 8 GB cleared: 41 - 8 + 0.18m <= 64, so 0.18m <= 31, m <= 172.22 min (~2h 52m). Extra time = 172.22 - 127.78 ≈ 44.4 more minutes.\n7. Prediction: 0.06 is one-third of 0.18, so about 3x longer. Check: 0.06m <= 23, so m <= 383.33 min (~6h 23m). 383.33 / 127.78 ≈ 3.0. Confirmed.",
